  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday) \nIssued at 1158 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge that is currently plaguing the region is \nanticipated to briefly weaken and shift westward Tuesday into \nWednesday, before restrengthening over the Desert Southwest during\nthe daytime on Thursday. As it shifts west, eastern OK and \nnorthwest AR will be caught in a northwest flow aloft. This will \ndo two things...allow for potential mesoscale features and \nboundaries to move into or through the area, and decrease the \npotent subsidence currently occurring. As a result of both of\nthese, temperatures will gradually cool off slightly, closer \nseasonal average from mid to late week. \n\nModels and ensembles show a series of shortwave troughs moving\nacross the Midwest Region between Wednesday and Friday,\nintroducing isolated to widely scattered (20-60%) showers and \nthunderstorms each day, mainly for the northeastern half of the \nforecast area. At the surface, the stalled frontal boundary from\nTuesday night will lift north as a warm front on Thursday as an \narea of low pressure develops across the High Plains and lifts \nnortheast across the Great Plains Thursday evening and night. As \nit lifts further north and east, it will drag a weak cold front \nthrough the entire forecast area sometime Friday \nafternoon/evening, with exact timing still uncertain. Though not a\nton of precipitation is expected with the front, temperatures \nwill cool down closer to or even slightly below seasonal averages \n(highs in the low-mid 90s) Saturday, Sunday, and into Monday. \n\nMejia",
